<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peronism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1940s–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Justicialism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peronism, or Justicialism, is the Argentine political movement formed around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a> in the 1940s, resting on organised labour, economic nationalism, state-led industry and social welfare. Banned after Perón's fall in 1955, it remained the country's largest political force, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicialist-party">Justicialist Party</a> has produced presidents with very different policies, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a> to Néstor and Cristina Kirchner.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peronism.jpg" alt="Peronism" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No mencionado · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evita_y_Per%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Peronism gained popularity after earlier governments, such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hipolito-yrigoyen">Hipólito Yrigoyen</a>, failed to answer workers' pleas for better wages and conditions or to oppose the Argentine oligarchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a> became labour secretary after taking part in the 1943 military coup and was elected president in 1946. He introduced social programmes that benefited the working class, supported trade unions and called for greater state involvement in the economy. His wife Eva championed the rights of migrant workers, the poor and women, and was decisive in securing women's suffrage until her death from cancer in 1952.</p><p>Because of economic problems and political repression, the military overthrew Perón in 1955 and banned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicialist-party">Justicialist Party</a>. Open elections were held again only in 1973, when Perón was re-elected with 62 per cent of the vote. He died the following year, and his widow and vice president, Isabel, succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Peronism is defined by three flags: social justice, economic independence and political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> it took a third position, expressed in the phrase: we are neither Yankees nor Marxists. Perón grouped the sectors of society into organisations, among them the CGT for workers, and the trade unions remained a key part of the movement. Its measures included the expansion of workers' rights, women's suffrage, free tuition at public universities and a constitutional right to housing.</p><p>Scholars often classify it as left-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/populism">populism</a>. Perón himself wrote in 1973 that Peronism was a left-wing movement, though a Justicialist left and not a communist or anarchist one, and he named Christian socialism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> and Harold Laski among his inspirations. Historians also stress the influence of Catholic social teaching.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Peronism has spanned much of the ideological spectrum. Vandorism, or neo-Peronism, was the trade unionism that sought Peronism without Perón during the resistance after 1955. The orthodox sector demanded total attachment to Perón's presidencies, while the Revolutionary Tendency, mainly young, linked Peronism to socialist revolution. After the electoral defeat of 1983 the Peronist Renovation was formally constituted in 1985 by Antonio Cafiero, Carlos Grosso and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a>, joining Peronist values to liberal democracy. Menem, president from 1989 to 1999, broke with protectionist orthodoxy in favour of neoliberal policies, and Kirchnerism later formed around the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-kirchner">Néstor Kirchner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cristina-fernandez-de-kirchner">Cristina Fernández de Kirchner</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peronism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Peronism</b>, a mass movement combining nationalism, state-led industry and organised labour, shaped populist politics across Latin America, and Eva Perón remains one of the best-known Latin American women of the century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The words have stayed the same, but groups within <b>Peronism</b> have added their own verses, among them the Montoneros and Guardia de Hierro, and in recent years verses about the governments of Néstor Kirchner and Cristina Fernández de Kirchner.</blockquote><a href="/songs/peronist-march">Peronist March</a></li></ul></section>
